## Instrumentation redesign — one-shot onFirstNormalizedEvent proved useless

The first cut of this VTID's second diagnostic hook fired ONCE, on the
very first normalized Nova event of any kind (`onFirstNormalizedEvent`).
Deployed to staging and measured for real on the exact bimodal-slow
sessions this VTID exists to explain:

  reconnect:false sessions, both greeting_sent AND
  nova_first_normalized_event landing at ~762-766ms into the session,
  kind:"usage" — a connection-handshake accounting event Bedrock sends
  BEFORE the greeting prompt is even transmitted — while total_ms for
  those same sessions was 6.26s and 6.86s.

A one-shot hook firing on a pre-greeting handshake event tells us nothing
about the multi-second silence that follows greeting_sent. The design was
wrong, not just imprecise: whatever happens in the gap has to be observed
AFTER greeting_sent, and a single early snapshot can only ever catch
whatever races there first (which is always the handshake, not the
model's real response).

Redesigned before drawing any further conclusion or writing a fix:

- `onFirstNormalizedEvent` -> `onEarlyNormalizedEvent`, firing on each of
  the first `EARLY_EVENT_CAP` (12) normalized events, not just the first.
- `nova_first_normalized_event` (LatencyPhase) -> `nova_early_event`,
  fired multiple times in the same voice.latency.measured event, each
  mark's `detail` carrying `{kind, index}` so the dashboard sees a real
  ordered timeline instead of one point.
- `onFirstRawChunk` (byte-level, pre-normalization) kept as-is — it is
  still a genuinely useful, distinct signal (confirms Bedrock is sending
  bytes at all, independent of how our own normalizer classifies them).

